David Damerell wrote:
> Quoting  Jack Bolsen  <jbolsen@verizon.net>:
>
> >I disagree with this statement unless you are playing a gnome and you
> >want to try the protection racket. Leveling up makes good sense because
> >it will increase the likelihood of casting spells successfully and
> >increase power as well.
>
> And it does so faster than it increases monster difficulty?

Until you have the Amulet monster difficulty is determined by the
average of your depth level and XP level.  Go deep without
leveling and monters get generated that are meaner than you.
Level up and stay shallow and monsters get generated that are
easy for you to take out.

The reasons for playing the protection racket and the reasons
for leveling up don't have much overlap.  Pick one of the two
strategies, stick with it and be happy.  Neither approach is
wrong in any sense until much later in the game and not really
even then.

For a wizard higher XP levels mean lower spell failure rate,
higher damage caused by magic missile spell.  And it also
means more skill slots for dagger and attack spells.  So in the
case of wizard specifically, I do think that leveling up does
increase your meanness faster than it increase monster
difficulty but that's very much a matter of tastes on my part.



